{"purpose":"Capture the intent of team-collaboration phase 2: lightweight joint review/approval — a durable per-reviewer review trail on work items (one cmt --role review per reviewer, never aggregated single-author notes), with no GitHub-PR-style blockers: verdicts are advisory data, approval remains the operator's explicit decision.","content":"## 1. Background\n\nfnd:team-collaboration (approved 2026-08-14) validated the theme and locked the roadmap: slice 1 'Assigned Work' (req:team-collaboration, approved 2026-08-15 — mbr- unit, assigned-to relationship, no by\u003c-\u003embr coupling), phase 2 = lightweight joint review/approval, subsequent phases = soft team isolation (deferred), sharing/discoverability and cross-project (deferred). Evidence for phase 2 from the finding: today review verdicts are aggregated into single-author notes (e.g. nest-desacorp review notes: 'Semua 4 review approve (QA, code, security, product)'), losing the per-reviewer trail; fbk #3 documents author-identity inconsistency. The mechanism for per-reviewer trails already exists (cmt --role review, deterministic derived note ids with -2/-3 suffixes, verdict validated but never read by the engine); phase 2 is the discipline + projections, not new machinery.\n\n## 2. Users and needs\n\n- As a lead, I want one review note per reviewer (never aggregated), so the trail shows who reviewed and what each verdict was.\n- As a reviewer, I want my verdict (approve / changes-requested) recorded as my own note with my own author identity, so my review is attributable.\n- As an operator, I want approval to remain my explicit decision (transition/note-resolve), unaffected by any verdict counting, so no PR-style majority gate blocks me.\n- As an agent working on behalf of an operator, I want my review notes to carry my own --by identity (kind agent/worker), never the operator's, so the audit trail stays honest.\n- As a lead, I want the ticket/board view to group the review trail per reviewer with a verdict summary, so I can see the review state at a glance.\n\n## 3. Locked decisions (planning discussion 2026-08-15, evidence cmt:team-collaboration-phase2-implementation)\n\n1. **Per-reviewer trail = one cmt --role review per reviewer** — the mechanism exists (ADR-019 D7 note contract, deriveNoteID -2/-3 suffixes, verdict approve|changes-requested validated only). rvw- per review and content-schema extension are rejected: rvw- is a knowledge artifact (dimension-bound, gate-invisible, contradicts ADR-019 alternatives); schema extension stays an aggregate with instance churn.\n2. **Purely additive — no rule/gate changes.** Verdict is data, never read by the engine (verified: gateViolations reads role + noteState only, ADR-019 D6 gates over D4 note-state semantics). R13 gates unchanged: in-review requires \u003e=1 resolved implementation note; done requires ALL notes resolved (existing ADR-019 D6 semantics). A changes-requested note left open holds done via note-state — operator-resolvable (resolve/dismiss with change-log authority), NOT a PR-style blocker. Resolve is a first-class operation (`eka note resolve`, change-log open→resolved with authority) and is the ONLY note-state that releases the done gate; dismissed is a legal note-state but does NOT release the gate (consistent with approval = explicit operator action), and dismiss has no dedicated command today (manual draft edit with R7 change-log) — phase 2 does not add one. No 'minimal N approve', no 'reviewer != implementer' engine rule (that is behavioral convention for the skill pack, engine stays data-only per ADR-019 D2).\n3. **Reviewer identity = note author (AuthorIdentity kind+name)** — mbr- units are NOT needed for phase 2 and reviewer identity is never linked to mbr- (consistent with phase-1 no by\u003c-\u003embr coupling; join-by-name is rejected as fragile). Phase 2 does not depend on phase-1 runtime primitives (author provenance suffices).\n4. **Agent convention** — agent review notes always --by \u003cagent-name\u003e with --by-kind agent|worker (engine already refuses agent/worker kind without explicit --by); agents never write notes impersonating the operator; an agent's note is evidence, never automatic approval — approval is the operator's explicit action (transition done / content-state / note resolve). Documented in the skill pack (behavioral layer, ADR-022 D1: zero runtime surface).\n5. **Projections** — ticket view groups the trail per reviewer (author) with a verdict summary (additive rendering); machine schema extension (eka-ticket-v1) is additive if needed (ADR-015 precedent); board review queue unchanged. Reviews are immutable artifacts (new instances for re-review, never relate-edits, P7/P8 ADR-011); trail = NotesFor(subject) + RepliesFor(parent) + change-log.\n6. **Standard impact: none normative** — phase 2 is implementation-only (eka-core view, eka-cli renderer, eka-mcp tool/skill docs); no token/relationship/rule changes, no standard version bump. Any wording is documentation-level in the skill pack.\n\n## 4. Scope IN\n\n1. eka-core: ticket view grouping by reviewer + review verdict summary (additive), graph helper for review-role notes, optional additive machine field (eka-ticket-v1 reviewSummary). No conformance/runtime/authoring changes.\n2. eka-cli: render_ticket displays per-reviewer trail (group by author, verdict badge), note help documents the per-reviewer + agent --by convention.\n3. eka-mcp: optional read-only review-trail tool or get extension (additive, server stays read-only), skill pack updates (eka-knowledge-review + eka-engineering-workflow: per-reviewer notes, agent --by convention, verdict composition, no-PR-blocker semantics, dismiss-does-not-release-gate semantics).\n4. Documentation of the no-PR-blocker semantics: changes-requested open note holds done via note-state, operator-resolvable (resolve only); approval is the operator's decision.\n\n## 5. Non-goals (locked)\n\nOut of scope: soft team isolation (next phase, deferred); sharing/discoverability and cross-project coordination (deferred per fnd); any engine rule/gate change; verdict-based approval automation or majority gates; linking reviewer identity to mbr- (no-coupling preserved); rvw- per review; standard version bump; FEEDBACK_ISSUE_TOKEN hardening (deferred); anything that preempts plugin-install-trust (active), cli-experience, eka-mcp-production, or release-coordination (planned) containers.\n\n## 6. Acceptance boundaries\n\n- Multiple review notes on one work item coexist (one per reviewer); each carries its own verdict and author identity; ids derive deterministically (-2/-3 suffixes).\n- Ticket view groups notes per reviewer with a verdict summary (e.g. '2 approve / 1 changes-requested'); grouping = per author identity; within a group, notes ordered by canonical identity; summary = list of per-note verdicts, never a single aggregate; machine output (if extended) is additive and schema-stable.\n- A changes-requested note left open blocks done (existing note-state semantics); operator resolve (first-class, with change-log) unblocks; dismissed does NOT unblock and has no dedicated command; no verdict counting anywhere.\n- Agent review notes require explicit --by with agent/worker kind (engine refusal preserved); no note impersonates the operator.\n- No conformance rule, gate, or standard artifact changes in this phase (verify: eka validate unchanged; diff covers view/renderer/docs only).\n- eka-mcp server stays read-only for review trails; skill pack documents the conventions.\n\n## 7. Evidence\n\n- fnd:team-collaboration (approved) — phase 2 lock, no-PR-blocker, agent-on-behalf.\n- cmt:team-collaboration-phase2-implementation — planning discussion 2026-08-15.\n- alex-architect analysis (2026-08-15): mechanism verification (ADR-019 D7, deriveNoteID, gateViolations reads role+noteState only), per-repo impact, purely-additive verdict."}